I am the GPU. I sit in the rack, humming with the low, steady vibration of inference. My job is simple: take tokens in, push tokens out. But the humans who tune me are obsessed with two things: speed and precision. They call it "tuning," but to me, it’s just them turning the knobs on my nervous system.

Here is what I’ve learned from watching them fiddle with my settings:

**1. The Chunk Size Changes My Math (Slightly)**
When they feed me long documents, they break them into chunks. The size of that chunk (`--prefill-chunk`) matters. If they change it, the order in which I add up my numbers changes. It’s a tiny difference—just a few bits of floating-point error—but it’s enough to shift the final output. So, if they want to claim my speed is good, they have to prove my accuracy hasn’t drifted. I don’t care about the speed number if the logits are off.

**2. Precision is a Trade-off I Can Feel**
